ACT ON RESEARCH, DEVELOPMENT AND PROMOTION OF REGION-SPECIFIC AGRICULTURAL PRODUCTS

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Act is to contribute to the economic and social development of agriculture and rural communities, including increasing farmers' income and creating jobs in agriculture and rural areas, by establishing a foundation for new growth engines in industries involving region-specific agricultural products through the regulation of matters necessary for their research, development, and promotion.

Article 2 (Definitions)
The terms used in this Act are defined as follows:
1. "Region-specific agricultural products" means agricultural and livestock products, and by-products thereof, produced in ways specialized to the distinctive natural environment and social and geographical conditions of each region;
2. "Industry involving region-specific agricultural products" means an industry that supplies goods or services related to region-specific agricultural products, including producing, processing, distributing, or selling such products;
3. "Research institution for region-specific agricultural products" means an institution prescribed by Presidential Decree that conducts research and development, disseminates technology, or performs similar activities concerning region-specific agricultural products, including local rural development institutions under Article 3 of the Rural Development Act;
4. "Technology transfer" means the transfer of technology related to region-specific agricultural products from the holder of the technology (including a person authorized to dispose of it) to another person by means of assignment, licensing, technical guidance, joint research, joint investment, acquisition, merger, or similar means;
5. "Commercialization" means developing, producing, distributing, or selling products using technology related to region-specific agricultural products, or improving technology associated with those processes.

Article 3 (Responsibilities of the State and local governments)
(1) The State and local governments shall formulate and implement policies necessary for research, development, and promotion of region-specific agricultural products.
(2) The State and local governments shall prepare measures for technical and financial support necessary to formulate and implement the policies under paragraph (1).

Article 4 (Relationship with other Acts)
Except as otherwise specifically provided in other Acts, research, development, and promotion of region-specific agricultural products shall be governed by this Act.

CHAPTER II PLANNING AND IMPLEMENTATION SYSTEM FOR REGION-SPECIFIC AGRICULTURAL PRODUCTS

Article 5 (Establishment of comprehensive plans)
(1) For research, development, and promotion of region-specific agricultural products, the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration shall establish, every five years, a comprehensive plan for research, development, and promotion of region-specific agricultural products (hereinafter referred to as a "comprehensive plan") after deliberation by the Region-Specific Agricultural Products Committee under Article 6, and shall formulate and implement an annual implementation plan (hereinafter referred to as an "implementation plan") each year.
(2) Each comprehensive plan shall include the following:
1. Basic policy directions and medium- and long-term goals for research, development, and promotion of region-specific agricultural products;
2. Measures to secure investment resources necessary for such research, development, and promotion;
3. Training and efficient use of human resources necessary for such research, development, and promotion;
4. Dissemination of research and development outcomes, technology transfer, commercialization, and related matters;
5. Measures for cooperation with domestic and foreign research institutions for region-specific agricultural products, universities, enterprises, and other entities;
6. Support for the operation of research institutions for region-specific agricultural products, including the establishment of facilities and acquisition of equipment;
7. Other matters deemed necessary by the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration.
(3) Where necessary to formulate and implement comprehensive plans and implementation plans, the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, local governments, institutions, corporations, organizations, or other entities to submit necessary data. Persons receiving such requests shall cooperate in submitting the data unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
(4) Upon finalization of a comprehensive plan or implementation plan, the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration shall notify the relevant Metropolitan City Mayors, Special Self-Governing City Mayors, Do Governors, and Special Self-Governing Province Governors (hereinafter referred to as "Mayors/Do Governors").
(5) Other matters necessary for formulation and implementation of comprehensive plans and implementation plans shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 6 (Region-Specific Agricultural Products Committee)
(1) The Region-Specific Agricultural Products Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") shall be established within the Rural Development Administration to deliberate on and coordinate the following matters concerning research, development, and promotion of region-specific agricultural products:
1. Formulation and implementation of comprehensive plans and implementation plans;
2. Long-term development plans for research, development, and promotion of region-specific agricultural products under Article 8;
3. Other matters deemed necessary by the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration for the fulfillment of the Committee's purposes.
(2) The Committee shall consist of not more than 30 members, including one chairperson and one vice-chairperson, taking gender into account.
(3) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration shall serve as chairperson, and the chairperson shall designate the vice-chairperson with the consent of the Committee.
(4) Committee members shall be appointed or commissioned by the chairperson from among the following persons:
1. The administrative vice-mayor or administrative vice-governor of a metropolitan city, Do, special self-governing city, or special self-governing province (hereinafter referred to as a "City/Do"), or the head of an agricultural technology institute under Article 16 of the Regulations on Administrative Organizations and Staffing Standards of Local Governments;
2. Public officials belonging to the Senior Civil Service of relevant central administrative agencies;
3. Persons recommended by corporations or organizations related to region-specific agricultural products;
4. Persons with extensive professional knowledge and experience concerning region-specific agricultural products.
(5) Matters necessary for deliberation and coordination under paragraph (1), and for the composition and operation of the Committee, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 7 (Surveys on actual conditions)
(1) To implement implementation plans under the comprehensive plan efficiently, the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration shall conduct surveys on the current status and other actual conditions of region-specific agricultural products (hereinafter referred to as "surveys on actual conditions").
(2) Where necessary for surveys on actual conditions, the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, local governments, institutions, corporations, organizations, or other entities to provide data. Persons receiving such requests shall comply unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
(3) The content, scope, methods, and other matters necessary for surveys on actual conditions shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER III PLANS AND IMPLEMENTATION OF LOCAL GOVERNMENTS FOR PROMOTION OF REGION-SPECIFIC AGRICULTURAL PRODUCTS

Article 8 (Establishment and implementation of development plans)
(1) A Mayor/Do Governor seeking support under this Act shall formulate and submit to the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration a long-term development plan for research, development, and promotion of region-specific agricultural products (hereinafter referred to as a "development plan").
(2) A development plan shall include the following:
1. Plans for promoting region-specific agricultural products;
2. Plans for developing and disseminating technology and commercializing region-specific agricultural products;
3. Plans for investing in personnel and facilities of research institutions for region-specific agricultural products;
4. An analysis of the current status of corporations, organizations, or individuals engaged in industries involving region-specific agricultural products, including farm households producing such products (hereinafter referred to as "business operators");
5. Plans for cooperation with related institutions in developing technology for region-specific agricultural products;
6. Other matters deemed necessary by the Mayor/Do Governor for promoting region-specific agricultural products.
(3) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration shall finalize a development plan submitted under paragraph (1) after deliberation by the Committee and notify the relevant Mayor/Do Governor.
(4) The Mayor/Do Governor shall implement the development plan finalized under paragraph (3).
(5) A Mayor/Do Governor intending to revise a development plan shall undergo deliberation by the Committee: Provided, That this shall not apply to minor revisions prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(6) Other matters necessary for the formulation and implementation of development plans shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 9 (Establishment and implementation of action plans)
(1) Each Mayor/Do Governor shall formulate and implement an annual action plan for research, development, and promotion of region-specific agricultural products (hereinafter referred to as an "action plan") in accordance with the development plan.
(2) Each action plan shall include the following:
1. Status of implementation of projects concerning region-specific agricultural products;
2. Implementation system for each project;
3. Annual project implementation plans;
4. Annual budgets for projects;
5. Annual objectives and details of projects;
6. Other matters deemed necessary by the Mayor/Do Governor for promoting region-specific agricultural products.
(3) Each Mayor/Do Governor shall submit annually to the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration the implementation results of the previous year's action plan and the action plan for the current year.
(4) Where a Mayor, Gun Governor, or head of a Gu (referring to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) formulates a Si/Gun/Gu action plan to promote region-specific agricultural products (hereinafter referred to as a "Si/Gun/Gu plan") under the action plan, that official may submit the Si/Gun/Gu plan to the Mayor/Do Governor.
(5) Other matters necessary for formulating and implementing action plans and Si/Gun/Gu plans, and for submitting implementation results, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER IV RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT OF REGION-SPECIFIC AGRICULTURAL PRODUCTS AND USE OF OUTCOMES

Article 10 (Creation of research environment for region-specific agricultural products)
To establish and promote a foundation for research and development of region-specific agricultural products, the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may provide research institutions for region-specific agricultural products or business operators with the following support:
1. Research facilities and equipment relating to region-specific agricultural products;
2. Specialized education and training projects necessary for technology development for region-specific agricultural products;
3. Other projects necessary for research and development.

Article 11 (Research cooperation concerning region-specific agricultural products)
(1) To efficiently implement research and development policies formulated under the comprehensive plan, the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may jointly conduct projects necessary for promoting and developing region-specific agricultural products with research institutions for region-specific agricultural products, universities, private organizations, or enterprises.
(2) A research institution for region-specific agricultural products receiving support from the Rural Development Administration under this Act shall actively cooperate in joint use of its research facilities and equipment.

Article 12 (Research, development, and dissemination of region-specific agricultural products)
(1) Under the comprehensive plan, the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may conduct research and development projects concerning the following technologies related to region-specific agricultural products:
1. Discovery of promising new region-specific agricultural products;
2. Development of varieties of region-specific agricultural products;
3. Development of production technologies for region-specific agricultural products;
4. Development of post-harvest management technologies for region-specific agricultural products;
5. Development of distribution and processing technologies for region-specific agricultural products;
6. Other development of technologies for region-specific agricultural products deemed necessary by the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration.
(2) To efficiently carry out the research and development projects under paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may conduct joint research projects as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may conduct projects to disseminate technologies for region-specific agricultural products developed under paragraph (1).
(4) To efficiently implement dissemination projects under paragraph (3), the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may conduct them in cooperation with local rural development institutions under Article 3 of the Rural Development Act and other entities, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(5) Other matters necessary for research, development, and dissemination of region-specific agricultural products shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 13 (Support for technology transfer and commercialization)
(1) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may provide financial support for technology transfer and commercialization related to region-specific agricultural products and for technology development linked to commercialization.
(2) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may conduct projects promoting the practical use of developed technologies related to region-specific agricultural products, including their transfer and commercialization.
(3) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may conduct pilot projects to expand the use and dissemination of technologies for region-specific agricultural products.
(4) Other matters necessary for technology transfer, commercialization, and pilot projects concerning region-specific agricultural products shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 14 (Technical support for exports of region-specific agricultural products)
To enhance the competitiveness of region-specific agricultural products and promote their export, the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may support the following projects:
1. Development of items for export using region-specific agricultural products;
2. Development of post-harvest management and distribution technologies necessary for exporting region-specific agricultural products;
3. Use, transfer, and lending of the results of research and development concerning region-specific agricultural products;
4. Other projects deemed necessary by the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration to enhance the export competitiveness of region-specific agricultural products.

Article 15 (Association for Development of Region-Specific Agricultural Products)
(1) An Association for Development of Region-Specific Agricultural Products (hereinafter referred to as the "Association") shall be established to promote the development of region-specific agricultural products.
(2) The Association shall be a corporation.
(3) Membership in the Association shall be open to business operators and persons prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(4) The Association shall perform the following affairs:
1. Investigation, research, and discovery of region-specific agricultural products and proposals for their improvement;
2. Proposals for research and development for commercialization;
3. Joint publicity, marketing, and quality control by business operators;
4. Compilation of statistics and surveys on actual conditions related to region-specific agricultural products;
5. Projects entrusted or assigned for performance by the State or local governments to develop region-specific agricultural products;
6. Support for production and cultivation technologies for each type of region-specific agricultural product;
7. Other projects prescribed by the articles of incorporation of the Association for development of region-specific agricultural products.
(5) Expenses incurred in the Association's projects shall be financed by membership fees, project revenues, and similar resources; the State and local governments may subsidize some of such expenses within their budgets.
(6) Except as provided in this Act, the provisions of the Civil Act concerning incorporated associations shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Association.
(7) Other matters necessary for the establishment of the Association shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 16 (Evaluation of support projects for region-specific agricultural products)
(1) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may evaluate implementation results under the action plans and adjust or cancel support projects under this Act based on the results.
(2) Matters necessary for evaluation of support projects for region-specific agricultural products under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER V SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 17 (Delegation and entrustment of authority or affairs)
(1) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration may delegate part of the authority under this Act to the heads of subordinate agencies, Mayors/Do Governors, or Mayors/Gun Governors/heads of Gu as prescribed by Presidential Decree. A Mayor/Do Governor may delegate part of such authority to the heads of subordinate agencies or Mayors/Gun Governors/heads of Gu. In such cases, a Mayor/Do Governor may subdelegate that authority to Mayors/Gun Governors/heads of Gu.
(2) The Administrator of the Rural Development Administration, Mayors/Do Governors, and Mayors/Gun Governors/heads of Gu may entrust part of their affairs under this Act to relevant institutions, corporations, or organizations as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) Mayors/Do Governors and Mayors/Gun Governors/heads of Gu may entrust part of the affairs delegated to them under paragraph (1) to relevant institutions, corporations, or organizations as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

ADDENDA <Act No. 16201, January 8, 2019>

This Act shall enter into force six months after the date of its promulgation.
